  "reasoning": "The incident started with a manual FLUSHALL on the catalog cache at 09:24:00 by pmoreira, which caused a sharp drop in cache hit rate from around 0.93-0.95 to 0.05 by 09:59:00, indicating the cache was effectively empty after the flush (changes.jsonl:1, metrics/cache.csv:26-61). This led to a surge in origin queries per second from ~20-25 to over 600 at 09:27, saturating the database connections and triggering the DbConnectionsSaturated alert at 09:27 (alerts.jsonl:1, metrics/cache.csv:27-29). The cache hit rate continued to decline, causing many cache misses and recomputations logged as warnings for the catalog:index key with high concurrency (logs/app.jsonl:789, 930, 1089, etc.). The increased load on the database caused many 'too many connections' errors in the db-proxy service starting at 09:25 and continuing throughout the incident (logs/app.jsonl:797, 937, 1077, etc.). HTTP metrics show increased latency and error rates starting at 09:25, peaking around 09:54, consistent with backend saturation (metrics/http.csv:27-56). The incident appears to be caused by the manual cache flush leading to a cache cold start, causing a surge in origin queries that saturated the database connections and degraded service performance. There is no evidence of timeouts or failures unrelated to connection saturation. The main open question is whether the cache flush was necessary and if mitigation strategies like gradual cache warming or connection pool scaling were considered.",
